For the Risk Control evidence, I suggest using INC34965053 as the July/August example.

The incident followed the 29 July MQS release, where MQS introduced the data-loss mitigation change and precomp-request was added as the 7th BICoE message type. On 30 July, an unexpected MQS header-format change caused a BICoE ingestion issue, so incoming data was temporarily retained while the fix was applied.

During the recovery period, on 3 August, incoming MQS volume reportedly increased to around 70m messages compared with a normal maximum of ~5m. BICoE was not able to consume/process the full incoming volume within the normal window, and for the captured population we observed around 1m orphan records, showing a significant deterioration in message completeness.

For the monitoring evidence, please check the Radar Live – BICoE dashboard:

MQS & Inter-Orphans → MQS Day Wise Orphans

If you filter around 30 Jul – 3 Aug 2026, the dashboard clearly shows the abnormal orphan spike on 3 Aug compared with the normal baseline.

This should provide the evidence chain you need:

Monitoring detected the issue → INC34965053 raised → business communication issued → impact visible in the BICoE dashboard.

